# IFN-111 (was IFN-107, PR #64): send https://www.ifn.community/{path}/ to
# the canonical apex URL in a single 301.
#
# Canonical URLs:
#   https://ifn.community/          homepage (trailing slash)
#   https://ifn.community/{path}    every other path (no trailing slash)
#
# THESE RULES ARE INERT WHILE ifn.community IS THE PRIMARY DOMAIN.
#
# Netlify's automatic redirect between a primary domain's apex and its www
# counterpart runs before _redirects and netlify.toml are consulted, and no
# rule can override it (Netlify support forum; the redirect docs also say
# "You cannot use a redirect rule to add or remove a trailing slash" and that
# edge nodes normalize URLs before rules are matched). That automatic redirect
# keeps the path as requested, so today production does:
#
#   https://www.ifn.community/about/  -301->  https://ifn.community/about/
#   https://ifn.community/about/      -301->  /about
#
# The second hop is Netlify's static file handling, not a rule:
# scripts/prerender.mjs writes flat files (dist/about.html, not
# dist/about/index.html), and for a flat file Netlify answers the slashed
# form with a 301 to the unslashed one. That layout is deliberate; see the
# comment above `outPath` in scripts/prerender.mjs.
#
# The rules below only start matching once www.ifn.community is no longer
# auto-redirected, i.e. once ifn.community and www.ifn.community are both
# domain ALIASES of the site (Netlify's documented workaround is to make a
# different domain primary). At that point they are the only thing sending
# www to the apex, so they must cover every www path, and they do: the
# one- and two-segment rules drop a trailing slash on the same hop, and the
# splat catches the homepage, slash-less paths and anything deeper.
#
# Why this is safe both before and after that dashboard change:
#   - Every rule is scoped to the www host and points at the apex host, so
#     no rule can match its own destination (no loop), and the apex host,
#     deploy previews (*.netlify.app) and branch deploys never match.
#   - The apex keeps its existing behavior: /api/* rewrite, the explicit
#     200 routes and the 404 catch-all in netlify.toml are untouched.
#   - www /api/* requests get the same 301 to the apex that the automatic
#     redirect gives them today. The site calls /api/* with relative URLs,
#     so its own requests are always on the apex already.
#
# Netlify processes this file (copied from public/ to dist/) before the
# [[redirects]] in netlify.toml, so the 404 catch-all there cannot shadow
# these rules.
#
# There are deliberately NO http:// rules. Netlify upgrades http to https
# on the same host at the edge before any rule runs (production shows
# http://www.ifn.community/about -> https://www.ifn.community/about as the
# first hop), so http rules could never match, and an apex http rule would
# be one scheme-matching quirk away from a redirect loop on the apex.
#
# Do not add same-host trailing-slash rules here. Netlify normalizes slashes
# before matching, so a same-host slash-strip rule would loop.
#
# Verify with scripts/check-redirects.sh (production) after any change here
# or to the Netlify domain settings.

# www, trailing slash, one and two segments (every current sitemap path)
https://www.ifn.community/:path/        https://ifn.community/:path        301!
https://www.ifn.community/:path/:sub/   https://ifn.community/:path/:sub   301!

# www, everything else: the homepage, no trailing slash, deeper paths
https://www.ifn.community/*             https://ifn.community/:splat       301!
